==Problem==

A sequence of squares is made of identical square tiles. The edge of each square is one tile length longer than the edge of the previous square. The first three squares are shown. How many more tiles does the seventh square require than the sixth?

<asy>
path p=origin--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle;
draw(p);
draw(shift(3,0)*p);
draw(shift(3,1)*p);
draw(shift(4,0)*p);
draw(shift(4,1)*p);
draw(shift(7,0)*p);
draw(shift(7,1)*p);
draw(shift(7,2)*p);
draw(shift(8,0)*p);
draw(shift(8,1)*p);
draw(shift(8,2)*p);
draw(shift(9,0)*p);
draw(shift(9,1)*p);
draw(shift(9,2)*p);</asy>

<imath>\text{(A)}\ 11 \qquad \text{(B)}\ 12 \qquad \text{(C)}\ 13 \qquad \text{(D)}\ 14 \qquad \text{(E)}\ 15</imath>

==Solution 1==

For all positive integers <imath>n</imath>, the <imath>n^{\text{th}}</imath> square has side length <imath>n</imath> and thus consists of <imath>n^2</imath> tiles.

The seventh square thus has <imath>7^2 = 49</imath> tiles while the sixth square has <imath>6^2 = 36</imath> tiles.  Therefore, the seventh square has <imath>49-36=\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 13}</imath> more tiles than the sixth square.

==Solution 2==

As in solution 1, the sixth square has side length <imath>6</imath> and the seventh square has side length <imath>7</imath>.  A square of side length <imath>6</imath> may be transformed into a square of side length <imath>7</imath> by adding a row of <imath>6</imath> tiles below, a column of <imath>6</imath> tiles to the right, and <imath>1</imath> tile directly below the new column and to the right of the new row, for a total of <imath>6+6+1=\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 13}</imath> added tiles.

==Solution 3==
As in solution 1, the seventh square as <imath>7^2</imath> tiles while the sixth square has <imath>6^2</imath> tiles.  Thus the seventh square has <imath>7^2 - 6^2 = (7+6)(7-6) = 13 \cdot 1 = \boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 13}</imath> more tiles than the sixth square.

~AkCANdo, edited by j314andrews

